 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
-- Since: 2026-09-11
-- Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
-- Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
-- Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
-  the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+### §check-fails-closed — an internal failure aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-23 (the maintainer overrode §check-fails-open, 2026-09-11)
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit. Large staged files
+  still only warn and exit 0.
+- Forbidden: dropping `-e`, and `cmd && action` as the last command of a loop body or function (use `if`); under
+  `-e` with `pipefail` a false test there fails the whole pipeline.
+- Rejected: §check-fails-open (`set -uo pipefail` without `-e`, always `exit 0`). It was overridden by the maintainer
+  on 2026-10-23. It had been adopted after a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check under
+  `set -e` and blocked every commit for a day (2026-09-10). The `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` guard covers that case.
+- Reason: the maintainer wants the check's own failures to stop the commit instead of passing silently.
